$DEF,REM,;$DEF,-,<$DEC,$BAR,-,$BIN,~1;,$BIN,1;;;>;$DEF,--,<$DEC,$BAR,-,$BIN,~1;,$BIN,2;;;>;
There is a strip of cells numbered 1 to N. Each move either places
a chip on a cell (when the cell is empty) or removes a chip from a cell
(if there was one). Only cell 1 or the cell immediately following the first non-empty
cell can be changed. Initially, the strip is empty. One need to fill all the cells
on the strip.

The sequence of moves required to fill the cell need to be outputted as the cell numbers.
Placing a chip on a cell is recorded as a positive number, and removing a chip as negative.
$REM,<
# Python solution
def put(n):
    if n:
        put(n-1)
        print(n, end = " ")
        get(n-1)

def put2(n):
    if n:
        if n == 1:
            print(n, end = " ")
        else:
            put(n-1)
            print(n, end = " ")
            put2(n-2)

def get(n):
    if n:
        put(n-1)
        print(-n, end = " ")
        get(n-1)
put2(int(input()))
>;$DEF,put,<$~1,~1,$DEF,~1,<$put,$-,~1;; ~1 $get,$-,~1;;>;$DEF,0,;;>;$DEF,get,<$~1,~1,$DEF,~1,<$put,$-,~1;; -~1 $get,$-,~1;;>;$DEF,0,;;>;
$DEF,put2,<$~1,~1,$DEF,~1,<$put,$-,~1;; ~1 $put2,$--,~1;;>;$DEF,0,;$DEF,1,1;;>;
For instance, a strip with 3 cells results in the following moves:
$put2,3;

A 10 cell strip can be filled with the following moves:
$put2,10;
